---
/* The two-register page.
   Research question: what did the 1927 scan reveal about how the magazine was
   physically set, and how much of that can actually be established?
   The schematic states the discovery; the bars state its evidential reach.
   Register is never inferred from genre, content or author — where the scan
   gives no direct layout evidence the item is recorded as unknown, and unknown
   is drawn at full weight rather than left as empty space. */
import j from '../data/janghan.json';

const BX = 340, BW = 400, MAX = Math.max(j.issue1.items, j.issue2.items);
const w = (n) => (n / MAX) * BW;

const bars = [
  { label: j.issue1.label, sub: 'Issue 1 · 1927 scan survives', reg: j.issue1.register, total: j.issue1.items, y: 60 },
  { label: j.issue2.label, sub: 'Issue 2 · no 1927 scan known', reg: j.issue2.register, total: j.issue2.items, y: 152 },
];
const known1 = j.issue1.register.upper + j.issue1.register.strip;

const segs = [
  { k: 'upper', name: 'Upper block', fill: 'var(--b4)', op: 0.78 },
  { k: 'strip', name: 'Bottom strip', fill: 'var(--b1)', op: 0.9 },
  { k: 'unknown', name: 'Not established', fill: 'var(--void)', op: 0.45 },
];
---
<figure id="fig-registers">
  <div class="fig-head">
    <span class="fig-no">Figure 3 · The page itself</span>
    <p class="fig-title">
      Issue 1 was set in two registers — a main block above a running strip of short items
    </p>
  </div>

  <svg viewBox="0 0 760 268" role="img"
    aria-label={`Left: schematic of a page of 長恨 issue 1, showing a large upper block above a narrow running strip at the foot of the page. Right: stacked bars. Issue 1, of ${j.issue1.items} items, ${j.issue1.register.upper} are in the upper block, ${j.issue1.register.strip} in the bottom strip, and ${j.issue1.register.unknown} are not established. Issue 2, all ${j.issue2.items} items not established, because no 1927 scan survives.`}>

    {/* page schematic */}
    <text x="0" y="18" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.1" fill="var(--muted)">
      HOW THE PAGE IS SET
    </text>
    <rect x="14" y="34" width="176" height="212" rx="2" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
    <rect x="28" y="48" width="148" height="136" rx="2" fill="var(--b4)" opacity="0.78" />
    <text x="102" y="121" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="12.5"
      font-weight="600" fill="var(--paper)">upper block</text>
    <line x1="28" y1="196" x2="176" y2="196" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
    <rect x="28" y="204" width="148" height="28" rx="2" fill="var(--b1)" opacity="0.9" />
    <text x="102" y="222" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5"
      font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)">running strip</text>
    <text x="14" y="262" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--faint)">
      marked in the magazine’s own contents
    </text>

    {/* bars */}
    <text x={BX} y="18" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.1" fill="var(--muted)">
      HOW MUCH CAN BE ESTABLISHED
    </text>

    {bars.map((b) => {
      let acc = BX;
      return (
        <>
          <text x={BX - 108} y={b.y + 16} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13.5"
            font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)" lang="ko">{b.label}</text>
          <text x={BX - 108} y={b.y + 33} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--faint)">
            {b.sub}
          </text>
          {segs.map((s) => {
            const n = b.reg[s.k] || 0;
            if (!n) return null;
            const x0 = acc; acc += w(n);
            return (
              <>
                <rect x={x0} y={b.y} width={w(n)} height="26" fill={s.fill} opacity={s.op} />
                {w(n) > 26 && (
                  <text x={x0 + w(n) / 2} y={b.y + 18} text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--sans)"
                    font-size="12.5" font-weight="600"
                    fill={s.k === 'upper' ? 'var(--paper)' : 'var(--ink)'}>{n}</text>
                )}
              </>
            );
          })}
          <text x={BX} y={b.y + 46} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--faint)">
            {b.total} textual items
          </text>
        </>
      );
    })}

    {/* legend */}
    <line x1={BX} y1="222" x2="740" y2="222" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1" />
    {segs.map((s, i) => (
      <>
        <rect x={BX + i * 138} y="234" width="12" height="12" fill={s.fill} opacity={s.op} />
        <text x={BX + i * 138 + 18} y="244" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--muted)">
          {s.name}
        </text>
      </>
    ))}
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    The 1927 scan shows Issue 1 set in two registers: a main upper block, and a running strip of short items
    along the foot of the page that the magazine’s own table of contents marks separately. No prior inventory
    had recorded the layout. Its reach is limited and stated as such — position is established for
    {known1} of {j.issue1.items} Issue-1 items ({j.issue1.register.upper} upper,
    {j.issue1.register.strip} strip) and left unknown for {j.issue1.register.unknown}, because register is
    read off the page or not asserted at all. For Issue 2 no 1927 scan is known to survive, so register is
    unknown for all {j.issue2.items} items — an absence of evidence, not a finding about the magazine.
  </figcaption>

  <details class="chart-table">
    <summary>Data table</summary>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Issue</th><th scope="col">Upper block</th><th scope="col">Bottom strip</th>
          <th scope="col">Not established</th><th scope="col">Textual items</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {bars.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td lang="ko">{b.label}</td>
              <td class="num">{b.reg.upper}</td>
              <td class="num">{b.reg.strip}</td>
              <td class="num">{b.reg.unknown}</td>
              <td class="num">{b.total}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>
</figure>
